          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Aplastic anaemia and acute leukaemia can appear nearly identical on the complete</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Aplastic anaemia and acute leukaemia can appear nearly identical on the complete blood count. Which single investigation definitively distinguishes between them?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Peripheral blood smear examination</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The peripheral smear may offer clues such as presence or absence of blasts, but it does not definitively distinguish between the two. Bone marrow examination reveals marrow cellularity and architecture, providing the definitive answer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum lactate dehydrogenase level</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>LDH may be elevated in both conditions due to ineffective haematopoiesis or increased cell turnover. It does not reliably distinguish aplastic anaemia from leukaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow examination</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In aplastic anaemia the marrow is hypocellular and fat-replaced, whereas in acute leukaemia it is packed with blast cells. The CBC alone cannot make this distinction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coagulation profile</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Coagulation studies may show abnormalities in either condition when platelets are very low. They do not establish whether the underlying pathology is marrow aplasia or malignancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is bone marrow examination. As stated in the narration, aplastic anaemia and leukaemia can look nearly identical on a blood count; only bone marrow examination reveals the crucial difference — hypocellular marrow versus blast infiltration."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Bone marrow examination is the definitive investigation — hypocellular, fat-replaced marrow indicates aplastic anaemia, while blast-packed marrow indicates leukaemia."
                }
              ],
