          "label": "<p>A patient with pancytopenia undergoes bone marrow aspiration that yields a dry t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with pancytopenia undergoes bone marrow aspiration that yields a dry tap. The peripheral smear shows teardrop-shaped red blood cells, and there is palpable splenomegaly. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these findings?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aplastic anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Aplastic anaemia can produce a dry tap, but the smear shows normocytic normochromic cells without teardrop forms. Crucially, the absence of organomegaly is a hallmark negative finding in aplastic anaemia — splenomegaly argues against it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Megaloblastic anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Megaloblastic anaemia produces a hypercellular marrow with megaloblasts, macrocytic red cells, and hypersegmented neutrophils on the smear. It does not cause a dry tap or teardrop cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aleukemic leukaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In aleukemic leukaemia, blasts are present in the bone marrow even if sparse on the peripheral smear. A dry tap with teardrop cells and splenomegaly is not the typical pattern of this condition.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Myelofibrosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Myelofibrosis characteristically produces a dry tap due to dense marrow fibrosis, teardrop-shaped red cells (dacrocytes) that form as cells squeeze through the fibrotic marrow, and splenomegaly from extramedullary haematopoiesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is myelofibrosis. A dry tap occurs because dense fibrosis prevents fluid aspiration. Teardrop cells form as red cells squeeze through the fibrotic marrow. Splenomegaly results from extramedullary haematopoiesis. Aplastic anaemia can also yield a dry tap, but without teardrop cells or splenomegaly."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The combination of a dry tap, teardrop cells, and splenomegaly is the hallmark triad of myelofibrosis. Recognising this pattern is essential when evaluating pancytopenia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
