          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old man's bone marrow aspirate yields a dry tap. The trephine biopsy s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old man's bone marrow aspirate yields a dry tap. The trephine biopsy shows 20 percent cellularity with fat replacing haematopoietic tissue. There are no blasts, no fibrosis, and no abnormal infiltration. What is the most accurate interpretation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal cellularity for a 30-year-old</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>For a 30-year-old, expected cellularity is approximately 100 minus 30 = 70 percent. A cellularity of 20 percent is severely below normal for this age.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypercellular marrow suggesting a myeloproliferative disorder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Twenty percent cellularity is markedly below the expected 70 percent for this age. Myeloproliferative disorders produce hypercellular marrows, not hypocellular ones.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe aplastic anaemia — expected cellularity is approximately 70 percent</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Expected cellularity = 100 minus 30 = 70 percent. The observed 20 percent (below the 25 percent threshold) with fat replacement and no abnormal cells meets the criteria for severe aplastic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Myelofibrosis confirmed by the dry tap</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Although a dry tap can occur in myelofibrosis, the trephine here explicitly shows no fibrosis. Fat replacement without fibrosis points to aplastic anaemia, not myelofibrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is severe aplastic anaemia. Applying the rule: expected cellularity = 100 minus 30 = 70 percent. The observed 20 percent is below the 25 percent threshold for severe disease. The trephine shows fat replacing haematopoietic tissue with no fibrosis or abnormal cells, confirming the diagnosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The age-adjusted cellularity rule (100 minus age) reveals that 20 percent is severely hypocellular for a 30-year-old. Fat-replaced marrow without blasts, fibrosis, or infiltration confirms severe aplastic anaemia."
                }
              ],
